CONCLUSIONS / RECOMMENDATION (not hardware test results)

1. No new ChipIdea dual-role engine or Qualcomm HS PHY driver is indicated by
   this investigation. The missing host-power component is an SMB1360-specific
   regulator provider, with correct initialization, protection and charger
   interaction. The missing cable-detection component is a real VBUS signal
   delivered to both controller and PHY. GPIO110 already supplies ID evidence.
2. Keep the initial peripheral-only series unchanged. Changing dr_mode alone
   can select host logic without creating any VBUS source: the controller's
   regulator is optional. Probe success or a role attribute is not proof of OTG.
3. Prefer the existing split-extcon path for the smallest follow-up:
   controller extcon[0] = proved VBUS provider; extcon[1] = GPIO110 ID provider;
   PHY extcon[0] = the same proved VBUS provider; controller vbus-supply = actual
   SMB1360 boost regulator after that driver/binding exists. These are logical
   relationships, not a proposed ready-to-apply DTS or invented phandles.
4. First try to establish whether the existing PM8916 USBIN detector is actually
   wired to this connector. If proved, reuse its standalone extcon and avoid
   implementing SMB1360 VBUS-presence publication merely for redundancy. If not,
   SMB1360 USBIN status/IRQ is the vendor-backed sensing path to implement.
5. A switch to usb-role-switch is an architectural alternative, not a solution
   for missing physical sensing or 5V output. It would still need a real detector
   and consistent PHY session-valid updates, so it is not the minimal path here.
6. Scope "OTG" initially as automatic ID-cable host/peripheral use. The shared
   MSM8939 DTS disables ADP/HNP/SRP. Those protocol features/certification are not
   established by source support for dual-role operation or by enumeration.

