Controller and bindings (all paths at subject baseline):
- arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8939.dtsi:2161-2199 describes qcom,ci-hdrc at
  0x078d9000 with ULPI qcom,usb-hs-phy-msm8916 / qcom,usb-hs-phy, default
  dr_mode="otg", and adp-disable/hnp-disable/srp-disable (2176-2179).
- drivers/usb/chipidea/Kconfig:3-33 selects EXTCON, ULPI bus and USB_ROLE_SWITCH;
  host requires USB_EHCI_HCD + USB_CHIPIDEA_HOST; gadget requires USB_GADGET +
  USB_CHIPIDEA_UDC; Qualcomm glue is USB_CHIPIDEA_MSM (42-44).
- core.c:951-964 tests DCCPARAMS host/device capability bits at runtime. This
  code is a capability check; no such runtime observation was made here.
- core.c:1138-1157 initializes host/gadget according to dr_mode. In peripheral
  mode it does not initialize the host role. core.c:674-686 only requests the
  optional regulator named "vbus" outside peripheral-only mode; -ENODEV becomes
  NULL rather than refusing host mode. Thus omission is not a protective gate
  once a board is switched to dr_mode="otg".
- Documentation/devicetree/bindings/usb/chipidea,usb2-common.yaml:89-102 defines
  two independent extcon phandles: USB/VBUS first, USB-HOST/ID second; 175-176
  defines vbus-supply. ci-hdrc-usb2.yaml:27,79-86 covers qcom,ci-hdrc and PHY.
- core.c:749-780 reads those cable states; 807-830 registers notifiers.
  otg.c:29-74 substitutes extcon states for OTGSC BSV/ID, 122-145 chooses role
  and connects/disconnects the gadget, 174-207 stops/starts roles on ID changes.
- host.c:38-64 controls the regulator via the EHCI port-power callback.
  host.c:159-170 selects that normal path unless TURN_VBUS_EARLY_ON is requested;
  ci_hdrc_msm.c:188-193 does not set that flag. No bespoke board USB driver is
  needed to replace this power-control consumer.
- otg.c:193-202 skips physical BSV-drain waiting when an external connector or
  role switch supplies session state. Therefore validate detector behavior during
  boost-off/discharge; do not publish stale self-sourced VBUS as a new B session.
- core.c:616-631 role-switch setter converts role requests into ID/BSV software
  states and invokes ci_irq; 1174-1184 registers usb-role-switch. A successful
  request is not electrical power-good or successful peripheral enumeration.
- ci.h:461-470 additionally gates the optional OTG FSM on HNP/SRP/ADP capability;
  the disabled SoC DTS flags keep simple ID switching separate from that FSM.

PHY and GPIO detector:
- phy-qcom-usb-hs.c:42-86 sets PHY mode; with an extcon it disables OTG
  comparators (ULPI_PWR_CLK_MNG_REG 0x88 bit0) and selects external VBUS-valid in
  device/OTG mode (ULPI_MISC_A 0x96 bit1), deselecting it in host mode.
- phy-qcom-usb-hs.c:91-105 sets/clears ULPI_MISC_A bit0 on EXTCON_USB; 157-165
  initializes and subscribes to that state; 257-261 gets phandle index 0.
  It has no role-switch notification path. Deleting extcon assumes native
  sensing instead, which is not proved for ferrari.
- ci_hdrc_msm.c:117-122 enables the controller's software session-valid control
  for extcon or role-switch; this does not manufacture correct cable evidence.
- arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8939-pm8916.dtsi:151-153 supplies PHY 1.8V from L7
  and 3.3V from L13. These PHY rails are not the connector's 5V VBUS source.
- Documentation/devicetree/bindings/phy/qcom,usb-hs-phy.yaml:66-70 permits those
  supplies and extcon. linux,extcon-usb-gpio.yaml:20-32 permits ID alone.
- drivers/extcon/extcon-usb-gpio.c:66-82 uses vbus=id when vbus-gpios is absent.
  A local, non-hardware truth-table calculation of that expression gave:
      physical condition         ID  real VBUS  reported USB  reported HOST
      disconnected               1      0           1              0
      powered B cable            1      1           1              0
      unpowered OTG adapter      0      0           0              1
      powered A-side             0      1           0              1
  Therefore ID-only does not distinguish unplugged from powered B cable.
- drivers/usb/common/usb-conn-gpio.c:79-90 repeats the same missing-VBUS fallback,
  then requests roles. 102-113 also controls an optional VBUS regulator.
  Replacing extcon with gpio-usb-b-connector does not fix the signal. Do not
  assign two independent consumers ownership of VBUS policy casually.

Independent PM8916 sensing, not charger enablement:
- arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm8916.dtsi:102-108 already has disabled pm8916_usbin,
  compatible qcom,pm8941-misc, reg 0x1300, usb_vbus IRQ <0 0x13 1 ...>.
- drivers/extcon/extcon-qcom-spmi-misc.c:60-73 reads IRQCHIP_STATE_LINE_LEVEL and
  publishes EXTCON_USB; no LBC charging configuration is needed for this driver.
  drivers/spmi/spmi-pmic-arb.c:656,921-935 resolves that to peripheral RT_STS
  offset0x10, i.e. PM8916 USBIN 0x1310 bit1. This is a possible physical detector,
  not proof of its wiring or health on this phone.
- Existing MSM8916 LG C50/Samsung GT5/UFI board users demonstrate software reuse,
  not a ferrari net connection. The vendor external-charger flag proves neither
  the USBIN connection nor valid transitions; see related vendor evidence note.
- Do NOT enable pm8916_lbc for sensing. drivers/power/supply/pm8916_lbc.c:94-111
  programs charging and sets CHG_EN|PSTG_EN; probe calls it at345-346. Moreover,
  at300-308 this baseline logs external-charger/DT errors without returning at
  those statements. It must not be treated as a passive detector or a guaranteed
  rejection of external-charger boards. No fix or enablement was made here.

Other charger drivers are patterns, not compatible devices:
- smb347-charger.c:1611-1624 matches SMB345/347/358, not SMB1360.
- qcom_smbx.c:1039-1042 matches PMI8998/PM660 (SMB2); qcom_smbb.c:1009-1012
  matches PM8226/PM8941. Their regulator/extcon integration is reusable design
  precedent, not permission to bind SMB1360 to their register maps.

SMALLEST FOLLOW-UP PLAN (requires separate implementation/hardware approval)

A. Resolve only power/sense evidence first. Obtain exact running Lineage source
   or treat the Xiaomi release as a reference, not identical firmware. Establish
   SMB1360 silicon/configuration, output rating, input/boost isolation, UVLO and
   discharge behavior from authoritative documentation or coordinated captures.
   Confirm GPIO110, SMB1360 STAT GPIO62, input-valid/UV transitions and possible
   PM8916 USBIN transitions with boost OFF. No probing or boot change is authorized
   by this note. Prefer existing driver observations; IRQ/status reads may
   acknowledge latched events, so blind I2C scans/dumps are not "passive" evidence.
B. Implement new SMB1360 device-specific binding/driver in a separate follow-up,
   with one owner for its I2C registers. Limit scope to safe OTG regulator control
   and needed input/fault status. Use regmap/regulator/extcon infrastructure, not
   a wholesale Android charger/fuel-gauge port or an unnecessary new role engine.
   Audit which initialization is actually required; do not depend silently on
   bootloader charger state or overwrite battery policy by copying hw_init.
   If PM8916 sense is proved, reuse it; otherwise publish SMB1360 input-valid as
   EXTCON_USB. A power_supply ONLINE property alone is not the notifier interface
   currently consumed by the controller and PHY. Preserve unrelated register bits,
   propagate I2C errors, handle UV/OTG-fail/overcurrent, keep a faulted output off,
   reconcile hardware disable with regulator software state, and validate safe
   remove/shutdown/error behavior without speculative retries.
C. Offline review/build: binding checks and dtbs_check for the follow-up; kernel
   build with host+gadget+PHY+chosen extcon+regulator enabled; mock/regmap tests of
   masking, initial state, IRQ decoding, input-present guard, failed accesses,
   fault shutdown, and enable/disable/refcount/error paths. Do not claim these
   tests prove 5V, physical routing or working role changes.
D. With Sam explicitly coordinating one phone session, agree a nonpersistent
   boot/recovery method first. Preserve the backed-up/erased-boot fastboot state;
   unlocked does not establish fuse state or permission to flash anything.
   Test in increasing-risk stages:
   1) boost disabled: no cable -> powered B cable -> unplug. Log ID, real input
      sensor, controller state and PHY session-valid; prove gadget attach/detach.
   2) only after electrical safety review: isolated/current-limited setup, no
      externally powered VBUS tied to boost. Measure voltage, current, rise and
      fall/discharge; start with a small known safe load. Command-bit readback
      alone is insufficient. Do not begin with a powered hub or a hard short.
   3) after stage2, use a separate experimental OTG DT with the real regulator
      and detector for low-power host enumeration/transfers, unplug/return to B
      device mode, repeated cycles, initial-cable state, suspend/resume and
      regulator-off on teardown. Record ID, VBUS source/sense, extcon, PHY,
      controller role, USB logs and load together. Test faults first offline;
      any physical protection test needs its own safe agreed fixture.
   Finalize the follow-up OTG DTS only after that evidence, not merely after
   a successful role request. Keep the bounded first-boot series separate.

REPORTED HARDWARE CONTEXT / LIMITS

